WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:06.440
Superman's history on the big screen spans
decades, starting way back in the serials

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:11.280
in 1948 and eventually culminating in the
acclaimed Richard Donner films that pretty

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:12.300
much everyone loved.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.800
But since Donner was fired from Superman II,
the past few decades haven’t been kind to

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.300
Superman's cinematic exploits.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:23.940
From the failed throwback adventure Superman
Returns, to the off-again, on-again Man of

00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:29.179
Steel sequel, there's some reason to believe
that DC may never actually make another standalone

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:30.660
Superman movie again.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.900
"Yahhhhhhhhh!!!"

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:36.300
Too Good

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:41.680
Being a goody two-shoes hero may have resonated
with earlier generations, but in today's crowded

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.460
superhero field, Superman falls behind a pack
of characters far more nuanced than the heroic super alien.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:52.460
Good stories need conflict, and while there's
plenty of superpowered bad guys to fight and

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.500
more than enough special effects to go around,
it’s the internal conflict that tends to

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:59.220
set a good movie apart from a mindless blockbuster.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:02.640
When it comes to Superman, Kryptonite isn't
his biggest weakness.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:03.640
It’s perfection.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.320
He's so good that it’s hard to really have
him struggle with an existential crisis — and

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:11.740
it's those kinds of stories that help audiences
connect with a character.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:15.800
Man of Steel tried, but ended up with a moody,
emo Superman, instead.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.540
"...when I'm good and ready."

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:20.740
Too Powerful

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:26.200
Marvel seemed to learn this lesson faster
than DC: Sometimes, your most famous characters

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.040
don’t quite work as leading men.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:29.860
In Marvel's case, it was Hulk.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.640
After two mediocre movies, they figured out
he works best against the biggest villains

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.520
in the biggest adventures, like the first
two Avengers films.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.500
Superman has a similar problem.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:44.040
Having a hero who's pretty much unstoppable
makes it hard to set real stakes.

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:48.990
Sure, he may get banged up and bruised, but
how many times can a bad guy whip out Kryptonite?

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:51.880
There’s not supposed to be that much of
the stuff just lying around.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.340
After seven movies, the most well-known danger
to Superman has been played out.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:58.060
Team-up trouble

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:01.260
The days of solo superhero flicks are numbered.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:05.680
Even the A-list heroes like Captain America
aren't alone in their franchises once you

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.040
get past the origin story, and almost everyone
has super pals tagging along.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.849
Heck, even Deadpool brought along Colossus
and Negasonic Teenage Warhead for the ride

00:02:14.849 --> 00:02:16.160
in his first solo outing.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:20.700
Comics are all about crossovers, so it’s
no surprise the movies have followed suit.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:26.540
The first sign that Superman may be relegated
to co-star status was the 2015 news that Man

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.640
of Steel 2 was reportedly put on "permanent
hold."

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.600
While rumors swirled in late 2016 that development
was back on, there's still much more speculation

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:36.720
than concrete info.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:42.080
It seems DC may be planning to just use Superman
as part of its larger, ensemble films.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.959
They can still tell Superman stories, but
Dawn of Justice's box office performance proved

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.520
he doesn’t have to carry a whole movie by
himself.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:50.880
Money Talks

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:56.060
A film studio’s decisions almost always
come down to money — specifically, how to

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:57.340
make as much of it as possible.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:02.560
And DC's Trinity of Wonder Woman, Batman,
and Superman are arguably three of the most

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.120
famous superheroes in the world.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:09.080
But of those three, Superman and Wonder Woman
have always played second fiddle to the Dark Knight.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:14.380
Put simply: Every Superman movie put together
has made around $2.3 billion dollars, while

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:19.120
just two of Christopher Nolan’s Dark Knight
movies cracked a billion dollars on their own.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:20.860
Yeah, that’s a lot of cash.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.580
"I'm only burning my half."

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:31.180
That makes Batman the fourth most successful
franchise in all of film, behind only Star

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.000
Wars, James Bond, and Marvel.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:38.280
Yes, Superman certainly factors into Warner's
plans, but Batman has always been the cash cow.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.780
Don’t expect that to change anytime soon.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:42.420
Packed Schedule

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:47.320
There’s also the fact that Warner Bros has
DC movies scheduled out through 2020, and

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:49.860
a solo Superman movie is nowhere to be found.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:54.460
Everyone from Cyborg, to the Flash, and even
the Green Lantern Corps are getting their

00:03:54.469 --> 00:03:58.639
own movies over the next few years — and
the first Green Lantern flick was pretty much

00:03:58.640 --> 00:03:59.320
a disaster.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.080
So, DC has more than enough on its plate without
trying to find something else for Superman

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.100
to do in the meantime.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.960
But if the studio ever does figure out how
to finally make another truly great solo movie

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:11.300
with the big blue boyscout?

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:13.880
Well...that'd be just super, man.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:15.060
Thanks for watching!

